{"cuisine/cameroonian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cameroonian-cuisine","n":"Cameroonian cuisine","c":"#e46a3f","crumb":[["Africa","cuisines#africa"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Cameroon","intro":[["Cameroonian cuisine is one of the most varied in Africa due to Cameroon's location on the crossroads between the north, west, and center of the continent; the diversity in ethnicity with mixture ranging from Bantus, Bamileke people, Bamoun, Bamenda people and Shuwa Arabs, as well as the influence of German, French and British colonization."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cameroonian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cameroonian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Precious Core","ref":"site/preciouscore-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"preciouscore.com","note":"Recipe blog by Precious Nkeih centered on Cameroonian dishes, with a companion YouTube channel.","url":"https://www.preciouscore.com/?p=22","suggested":true},{"name":"Immaculate Bites","ref":"site/africanbites-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"africanbites.com","note":"African and Caribbean recipes from Cameroon born cook Imma, with many Cameroonian dishes.","url":"https://www.africanbites.com/about","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Palm nut soup","dish/palm-nut-soup"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Crayfish","ingredient/crayfish"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."}],"cont":"African cuisine"},"cuisine/south-african-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/south-african-cuisine","n":"South African cuisine","c":"#e46a3f","crumb":[["Africa","cuisines#africa"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of South Africa","intro":[["South African cuisine draws from the culinary traditions of the various communities that inhabit the country. Among the indigenous peoples of South Africa, the Khoisan foraged over 300 species of edible food plants, such as the rooibos shrub legume, whose culinary value continues to influence South African cuisine. Subsequent encounters with Bantu pastoralists facilitated the emergence of cultivated crops and domestic cattle, which supplemented traditional Khoisan techniques of meat preservation. In addition, Bantu-speaking communities produced an extensive repertoire of ingredients and dishes, many of which are still consumed today in traditional settlements and urban entrepôts alike."],["South African cuisine is heavily based on meat consumption. A widely observed culinary tradition is braai, a social event in which South Africans gather to cook meat over an open fire. A popular South African snack is biltong, an air dried, seasoned meat."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"south-african-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_African_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":3,"rows":[{"name":"Drizzle and Dip","ref":"site/drizzleanddip-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"drizzleanddip.com","note":"South African recipes section of Cape Town food writer Sam Linsell's blog.","url":"https://drizzleanddip.com/category/recipes/cuisine/south-african/","suggested":true},{"name":"TASTE","ref":"site/taste-co-za","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"taste.co.za","note":"South African recipe collection from the Woolworths food magazine.","url":"https://taste.co.za/south-african-recipes","suggested":true},{"name":"The Cinnamon Jar","ref":"site/thecinnamonjar-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"thecinnamonjar.com","note":"South African favourites like bobotie and milk tart from Maretha, a South African home cook in the UK.","url":"https://thecinnamonjar.com/category/south-african-recipes/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"The South African Cookbook","ref":"cookbook/the-south-african-cookbook","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Nokx Majozi, 2026","note":"Recipes from Cape Town to Durban by a Durban raised chef formerly at Rosewood London.","url":"https://interlinkbooks.com/product/the-south-african-cookbook/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":4,"links":[["Bobotie","dish/bobotie"],["Fish and chips","dish/fish-and-chips"],["Hertzoggie","dish/hertzoggie"],["Malva pudding","dish/malva-pudding"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Dried fruit","ingredient/dried-fruit"],["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"],["Atlantic cod","ingredient/atlantic-cod"],["Fish","ingredient/fish"],["French fries","ingredient/french-fries"],["Fried fish","ingredient/fried-fish"],["Fries","ingredient/fries"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Apricot jam","ingredient/apricot-jam"],["Baking soda","ingredient/baking-soda"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Desiccated coconut","ingredient/desiccated-coconut"],["Margarine","ingredient/margarine"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Apricot","ingredient/apricot"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Within South African cooking","count":1,"links":[["Afrikaner cuisine","cuisine/afrikaner-cuisine"]]},{"h":"More from South Africa","count":1,"links":[["Indo-South African food","cuisine/indian-south-africans-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"African cuisine"},"cuisine/liberian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/liberian-cuisine","n":"Liberian cuisine","c":"#e46a3f","crumb":[["Africa","cuisines#africa"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Liberia","intro":[["Liberian cuisine refers to the cuisine of Liberia. It is centered on rice, cassava, plantain, yam, tropical fruits and vegetables (potatoes, greens, cassava leaf, okra, cabbage), as well as fish and meat. Liberian cuisine is also influenced by African Americans through Americo-Liberians and Caribbean food and recipes."],["Liberia also has a tradition of baking, including cornbread, sour bread, rice bread, banana bread, and cakes."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"liberian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liberian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]}],"cont":"African cuisine"},"cuisine/ningxia-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/ningxia-cuisine","n":"Ningxia cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in China","intro":[],"src":null,"wiki":"Ningxia cuisine","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ningxia-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ningxia_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/bush-tucker":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/bush-tucker","n":"Bush tucker","c":"#55b8ad","crumb":[["Oceania","cuisines#oceania"],["Australian cuisine","cuisine/australian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Ashmore and Cartier Is.","intro":[["Bush tucker, also called bush food, is any food native to Australia and historically eaten by Aboriginal Australians and Torres Strait Islanders, but it can also describe any native flora, fauna or fungi used for culinary or medicinal purposes, regardless of the continent or culture. Animal native foods include kangaroo, emu, witchetty grubs and crocodile. Plant foods include fruits such as quandong, kutjera, spices such as lemon myrtle and vegetables such as warrigal greens and various native yams."],["Traditional Indigenous Australians' use of bush foods has been severely affected by the British colonisation of Australia beginning in 1788 and subsequent settlement by non-Indigenous peoples. The introduction of non-native organisms, together with the loss of and destruction of traditional lands and habitats, has resulted in reduced access to native foods by Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people."],["Since the 1970s, there has been recognition of the nutritional and gourmet value of native foods by non-Indigenous Australians, and the bushfood industry has grown enormously. Kangaroo meat has been available in supermarkets since the 1980s, and many other foods are sold in restaurants or packaged as gourmet foods, which has led to expansion of commercial cultivation of native food crops."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"bush-tucker"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bush_tucker"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Australian cuisine","cuisine/australian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":3,"rows":[{"name":"Mabu Mabu","ref":"cookbook/mabu-mabu","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Nornie Bero, 2021","note":"Home cooking with native Australian ingredients by Torres Strait Islander chef Nornie Bero.","url":"https://publishing.hardiegrant.com/en-us/books/mabu-mabu-by-nornie-bero/9781743797280","suggested":true},{"name":"Native Ingredients Everyday","ref":"cookbook/native-ingredients-everyday","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Nornie Bero","note":"Nornie Bero's follow-up on using native Australian ingredients in everyday meals, from Hardie Grant.","url":"https://publishing.hardiegrant.com/en-gb/books/native-ingredients-everyday-by-nornie-bero/9781761452048","suggested":true},{"name":"The Australian Ingredients Kitchen: Simple Recipes Using Bush Foods","ref":"cookbook/the-australian-ingredients-kitchen-simple-recipes-using-bush-foods","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Bruno Dann and Tahlia Mandie","note":"Simple recipes built around Australian bush foods, from Hardie Grant.","url":"https://www.hardiegrantgift.com.au/books/australian-ingredients-kitchen-simple-recipes-using-bush-foods-9781761500510","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Australian cuisine","cuisine/australian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Oceanic cuisine"},"cuisine/papua-new-guinean-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/papua-new-guinean-cuisine","n":"Papua New Guinean cuisine","c":"#55b8ad","crumb":[["Oceania","cuisines#oceania"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Papua New Guinea","intro":[["The cuisine of Papua New Guinea comprises the traditional varied foods found in the eastern part of the New Guinea island. Approximately 80 percent  of the population is reliant on subsistence agriculture, so a large percentage of food energy and protein consumed in Papua New Guinea is produced locally. The staple foods in Papua New Guinea include root crops, bananas, and sago. Papua New Guinea's diet is largely vegetarian, especially in the Gulf and Highlands regions."],["Mumu is a traditional method of cooking large quantities of food throughout Papua New Guinea, as well as other islands in the Pacific. It consists of an earth oven that is filled with hot coal or stones, that may be placed in different orientations, and subsequently cooked for a lengthy period of time. Despite the presence of advent ovens in Papua New Guinea, mumu is still prevalent at the household level."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"papua-new-guinean-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Papua_New_Guinean_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Eat Pacific: The Pacific Island Food Revolution Cookbook","ref":"cookbook/eat-pacific-the-pacific-island-food-revolution-cookbook","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Robert Oliver (ed.), 2024","note":"Pacific Island Food Revolution cookbook with a Papua New Guinea section.","url":"https://masseypress.ac.nz/books/eat-pacific","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from Papua New Guinea","count":1,"links":[["Bougainville cuisine","cuisine/bougainville-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Oceanic cuisine"},"ingredient/candlenut":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/candlenut","n":"Candlenut","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 20 dishes across 5 cuisines","intro":[["Aleurites moluccanus, commonly known as candlenut, Indian walnut or, in Hawaii, kukui, is a tree in the spurge family Euphorbiaceae. It grows to about 30 m (98 ft) tall and produces drupe fruit."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-candlenut"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aleurites_moluccanus"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":5,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Arsik","dish/arsik"],["Ayam goreng","dish/ayam-goreng"],["Burgo (food)","dish/burgo-food"],["Coto makassar","dish/coto-makassar"],["Dengke mas naniura","dish/dengke-mas-naniura"],["Empal gentong","dish/empal-gentong"],["Gepuk","dish/gepuk"],["Gudeg","dish/gudeg"],["Konro","dish/konro"],["Manuk napinadar","dish/manuk-napinadar"],["Mie caluk","dish/mie-caluk"],["Nasi gandul","dish/nasi-gandul"],["Natinombur","dish/natinombur"],["Otak-otak","dish/otak-otak"],["Rendang","dish/rendang"],["Sambal tuktuk","dish/sambal-tuktuk"],["Sate klatak","dish/sate-klatak"],["Sate lilit","dish/sate-lilit"],["Sop saudara","dish/sop-saudara"],["Udang balado","dish/udang-balado"]]],["Acehnese cuisine","cuisine/acehnese-cuisine",[["Mie caluk","dish/mie-caluk"]]],["Balinese cuisine","cuisine/balinese-cuisine",[["Sate lilit","dish/sate-lilit"]]],["Javanese cuisine","cuisine/javanese-cuisine",[["Nasi gandul","dish/nasi-gandul"]]],["Padang food","cuisine/padang-food",[["Rendang","dish/rendang"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Galangal","ingredient/galangal"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Lemongrass","ingredient/lemongrass"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Turmeric","ingredient/turmeric"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Black pepper","ingredient/black-pepper"],["Bird's eye chili","ingredient/bird-s-eye-chili"],["Kencur","ingredient/kencur"],["Lime","ingredient/lime"],["Andaliman","ingredient/andaliman"],["Shallot","ingredient/shallot"],["Etlingera elatior","ingredient/etlingera-elatior"]]}]},"ingredient/fish":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/fish","n":"Fish","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 27 dishes across 35 cuisines","intro":[["A fish is an aquatic, anamniotic, gill-bearing vertebrate with a tough cranium to protect the brain. Many fish have fins that they use for directional stability and propulsion. Fish can be grouped into the more basal jawless fish and the more common jawed fish, the latter including all living cartilaginous and bony fish, as well as the extinct placoderms and acanthodians. Tetrapods are descended from fish, so in a break from the long tradition of grouping all fish into a single class (Pisces), modern phylogenetics views fish as a paraphyletic group that includes all vertebrates except tetrapods. To treat fish as a monophyletic group, one would have to classify tetrapods as a clade/subgroup of specialized fish, which would make \"fish\" and \"vertebrates\" synonyms. In English, the plural of \"fish\" is fish when referring to individuals and fishes when referring to species."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-fish"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fish"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":35,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Asam pedas","dish/asam-pedas"],["Lemper","dish/lemper"],["Pempek","dish/pempek"],["Pindang","dish/pindang"],["Satay","dish/satay"],["Semur (Indonesian stew)","dish/semur-indonesian-stew"]]],["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine",[["Bouillabaisse","dish/bouillabaisse"],["Bourride","dish/bourride"],["Pâté","dish/pate"],["Pottage","dish/pottage"]]],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine",[["Bouillabaisse","dish/bouillabaisse"],["Bourride","dish/bourride"],["Pâté","dish/pate"],["Pottage","dish/pottage"]]],["Malay cuisine","cuisine/malay-cuisine",[["Ikan bakar","dish/ikan-bakar"],["Lekor","dish/le