dried-fish"],["Engraulis","ingredient/engraulis"],["Papaya","ingredient/papaya"],["Taro","ingredient/taro"],["Palm sugar","ingredient/palm-sugar"],["Rice flour","ingredient/rice-flour"],["Vanilla","ingredient/vanilla"],["Okara","ingredient/okara"],["Peanut","ingredient/peanut"],["Corn starch","ingredient/corn-starch"],["Starch","ingredient/starch"]]},{"h":"Mentioned in","links":[["Amazonian Kichwa foodways","cuisine/amazonian-kichwa-foodways"],["Garifuna foodways","cuisine/garifuna-foodways"],["Guaraní foodways","cuisine/guarani-foodways"],["Maya cuisine","cuisine/maya-cuisine"],["Taíno heritage foodways","cuisine/taino-heritage-foodways"],["Wayuu foodways","cuisine/wayuu-foodways"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/f/f1/Manihot_esculenta_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-090.jpg","w":500,"h":646,"by":"Franz Eugen Köhler, Köhlers Medizinal-Pflanzen","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manihot_esculenta_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-090.jpg"}},"dish/eggs-sardou":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/eggs-sardou","n":"Eggs Sardou","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Louisiana Creole cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Eggs Sardou is a Louisiana Creole cuisine dish made with poached eggs, artichoke bottoms, creamed spinach and Hollandaise sauce. It is on the menu of many Creole restaurants in New Orleans, including Antoine's, where eggs Sardou was invented, and at Brennan's. Eggs Sardou is named for Victorien Sardou, a famous French dramatist of the 19th century, who was a guest in New Orleans when the dish was invented around the turn of the century (from 19th to 20th)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-eggs-sardou"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eggs_Sardou"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Louisiana Creole cuisine","cuisine/louisiana-creole-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":7,"links":[["Artichoke","ingredient/artichoke"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Engraulis","ingredient/engraulis"],["Hollandaise sauce","ingredient/hollandaise-sauce"],["Poached egg","ingredient/poached-egg"],["Spinach","ingredient/spinach"],["Tuber","ingredient/tuber"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Kenneth Temple: Cajun and Creole","ref":"site/kennethtemple-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"kennethtemple.com","note":"Cajun and Creole recipes from a New Orleans-based cook and video creator.","url":"https://kennethtemple.com/recipes/cajun-creole/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from Louisiana Creole cuisine","links":[["Antoine's","dish/antoine-s"],["Brennan's","dish/brennan-s"],["Clancy's","dish/clancy-s"],["Mosca's","dish/mosca-s"],["New Orleans hot sausage","dish/new-orleans-hot-sausage"],["Oysters Bienville","dish/oysters-bienville"],["Oysters Rockefeller","dish/oysters-rockefeller"],["Red beans and rice","dish/red-beans-and-rice"]]}]},"ingredient/tuber":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/tuber","n":"Tuber","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Tuber is a genus in the fungal family Tuberaceae, with estimated molecular dating to the end of the Jurassic period (156 Mya). It includes several species of truffles that are highly valued as delicacies."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-tuber"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tuber_(fungus)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Louisiana Creole cuisine","cuisine/louisiana-creole-cuisine",[["Eggs Sardou","dish/eggs-sardou"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Artichoke","ingredient/artichoke"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Engraulis","ingredient/engraulis"],["Hollandaise sauce","ingredient/hollandaise-sauce"],["Poached egg","ingredient/poached-egg"],["Spinach","ingredient/spinach"]]}]},"ingredient/thai-chili":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/thai-chili","n":"Thai chili","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Bird's eye chili or Thai chili is a chili pepper variety from the species Capsicum annuum that is native to Mexico. Cultivated across Southeast Asia, it is used extensively in many Asian cuisines. It may be mistaken for a similar-looking chili derived from the species Capsicum frutescens, the cultivar siling labuyo. Capsicum frutescens fruits are generally smaller and characteristically point upwards. In the Marianas and Guam, these are often called boonie peppers or Doni Sali, which can be a term for regional wild (technically feral) varieties. The variation between different varieties can be significant for regional dishes or visuals, such as the Thai ornamental varieties."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-thai-chili"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thai_chili"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Hmong cuisine","cuisine/hmong-cuisine",[["Hmong sausage","dish/hmong-sausage"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Galangal","ingredient/galangal"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Lemongrass","ingredient/lemongrass"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"]]}]},"dish/maccu":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/maccu","n":"Maccu","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Sicilian cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Maccu (also known as maccu di fave and sometimes referred to as macco) is a Sicilian soup with a dense texture and also a foodstuff that is prepared with dried and crushed fava beans (broad beans) and wild fennel as primary ingredients. Several dishes exist using maccu as an ingredient, such as bruschetta al maccù and maccu di San Giuseppe, the latter of which may be served on Saint Joseph's Day in Sicily."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-maccu"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maccu"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Sicilian cuisine","cuisine/sicilian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"More from Sicilian cuisine","links":[["Busiate","dish/busiate"],["Cassatella di Agira","dish/cassatella-di-agira"],["Crocchè","dish/crocche"],["Iris (pastry)","dish/iris-pastry"],["Panella","dish/panella"],["Pani câ meusa","dish/pani-ca-meusa"],["Parmigiana","dish/parmigiana"],["Pasta alla Norma","dish/pasta-alla-norma"],["Pasta con le sarde","dish/pasta-con-le-sarde"],["Pasta mollicata","dish/pasta-mollicata"],["Stigghiola","dish/stigghiola"]]}]},"dish/meat-jun":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/meat-jun","n":"Meat jun","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Korean cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Meat jun is a Korean fusion dish consumed in Hawaii, United States. It is also called \"beef jun\" outside of Hawaii. The dish is a variant of jeon (Korean pan-fried dishes) and descends from sogogi-jeon (소고기전). It consists of thinly sliced marinated beef dipped in an egg batter that is then pan-fried, or deep-fried, and typically served with rice, macaroni salad and banchan, like how many other plate lunches in Hawaii are served. Unlike typical beef jeon, in meat jun the beef is marinated. Meat jun is served as an everyday dish."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-meat-jun"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meat_jun"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Korean cuisine","cuisine/korean-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":5,"links":[["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Egg wash","ingredient/egg-wash"],["Marinade","ingredient/marinade"],["Soy sauce","ingredient/soy-sauce"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Maangchi","ref":"site/maangchi-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"maangchi.com","note":"Maangchi's Korean recipe site and YouTube channel, one of the best known English sources for Korean cooking.","url":"https://www.maangchi.com/","suggested":true},{"name":"Korean Bapsang","ref":"site/koreanbapsang-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"koreanbapsang.com","note":"Home-style Korean recipes from Hyosun, a Korean-American mom.","url":"https://www.koreanbapsang.com/category/recipes/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from Korean cuisine","links":[["Bap (rice dish)","dish/bap-rice-dish"],["Bibim-guksu","dish/bibim-guksu"],["Bibimbap","dish/bibimbap"],["Bosintang","dish/bosintang"],["Cheonggukjang","dish/cheonggukjang"],["Danji","dish/danji"],["Doenjang","dish/doenjang"],["Garak-guksu","dish/garak-guksu"],["Gochujang","dish/gochujang"],["Gopchang","dish/gopchang"],["Han Oak","dish/han-oak"],["Hwajeon","dish/hwajeon"],["Jijimi","dish/jijimi"],["Jokbal","dish/jokbal"],["Kim Jong Grillin'","dish/kim-jong-grillin"],["Korean restaurant","dish/korean-restaurant"],["Maekjeok","dish/maekjeok"],["Maeun-tang","dish/maeun-tang"],["Makchang","dish/makchang"],["Mandu-guk","dish/mandu-guk"],["Mandu-gwa","dish/mandu-gwa"],["Maum (restaurant)","dish/maum-restaurant"],["Meju","dish/meju"],["Morkovcha","dish/morkovcha"],["Parachute (restaurant)","dish/parachute-restaurant"],["Revelry (restaurant)","dish/revelry-restaurant"],["Scorched rice","dish/scorched-rice"],["Seon (food)","dish/seon-food"],["Sungnyung","dish/sungnyung"],["Toki (restaurant)","dish/toki-restaurant"],["Tteokbokki","dish/tteokbokki"],["Wakame","dish/wakame"],["Yakgwa","dish/yakgwa"],["Yukpo","dish/yukpo"]]}]},"dish/mexican-restaurant":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/mexican-restaurant","n":"Mexican restaurant","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Mexican cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Mexican cuisine consists of the food traditions of Mexico. Its earliest roots lie in Mesoamerican cuisine. Mexican cuisine's ingredients and methods arise from the area's first agricultural communities, such as those of the Olmec and Maya, who domesticated maize, created the standard process of nixtamalization, and established  foodways. Successive waves of other Mesoamerican groups brought with them their cooking methods. These included the Teotihuacanos, Toltec, Huastec, Zapotec, Mixtec, Otomi, Purépecha, Totonac, Mazatec, Mazahua, and Nahua. With the Mexica formation of the multi-ethnic Triple Alliance (Aztec Empire), culinary foodways became infused (Aztec cuisine)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-mexican-restaurant"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mexican_restaurant"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Mexican cuisine","cuisine/mexican-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Mexico in My Kitchen","ref":"site/mexicoinmykitchen-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"mexicoinmykitchen.com","note":"Mely Martínez's long-running blog of Mexican home-style recipes.","url":"https://www.mexicoinmykitchen.com/","suggested":true},{"name":"Pati Jinich","ref":"site/patijinich-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"patijinich.com","note":"Recipe archive from the Mexican-born chef and host of the PBS series Pati's Mexican Table.","url":"https://patijinich.com/recipes/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from Mexican cuisine","links":[["Acapulco Mexican Restaurant and Cantina","dish/acapulco-mexican-restaurant-and-cantina"],["Amigos (restaurant chain)","dish/amigos-restaurant-chain"],["Animalón","dish/animalon"],["Anna's Taqueria","dish/anna-s-taqueria"],["Baker's Drive-Thru","dish/baker-s-drive-thru"],["Barburrito","dish/barburrito"],["Birrieria La Plaza","dish/birrieria-la-plaza"],["Birrieria PDX","dish/birrieria-pdx"],["Boca Grande Taqueria","dish/boca-grande-taqueria"],["BurritoVille","dish/burritoville"],["Cafe Rio","dish/cafe-rio"],["California Tortilla","dish/california-tortilla"],["Cantina Mariachi","dish/cantina-mariachi"],["Carlos'n Charlie's","dish/carlos-n-charlie-s"],["Casa Bonita","dish/casa-bonita"],["Casa Enrique","dish/casa-enrique"],["Casa Vega","dish/casa-vega"],["Chevys Fresh Mex","dish/chevys-fresh-mex"],["Chi-Chi's","dish/chi-chi-s"],["Chico's Tacos","dish/chico-s-tacos"],["Chile con queso","dish/chile-con-queso"],["Chili con carne","dish/chili-con-carne"],["Chimichanga","dish/chimichanga"],["Chiquito (restaurant)","dish/chiquito-restaurant"],["Concha","dish/concha"],["Contramar","dish/contramar"],["Cosecha","dish/cosecha"],["Del Taco","dish/del-taco"],["Discada","dish/discada"],["District Taco","dish/district-taco"],["Don Pablo's","dish/don-pablo-s"],["El Adobe de Capistrano","dish/el-adobe-de-capistrano"],["El Bajío (restaurant)","dish/el-bajio-restaurant"],["El Chico (restaurant)","dish/el-chico-restaurant"],["El Coyote Cafe","dish/el-coyote-cafe"],["El Fenix (restaurant)","dish/el-fenix-restaurant"],["El Gallo Taqueria","dish/el-gallo-taqueria"],["El Tepeyac Café","dish/el-tepeyac-cafe"],["El Torito","dish/el-torito"],["Em (restaurant)","dish/em-restaurant"]]}]},"ingredient/coriander-leaf":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/coriander-leaf","n":"Coriander leaf","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Coriander (), or Coriandrum sativum, is an annual herb in the family Apiaceae. The leaves are known as cilantro () in the US. Most people perceive the leaves as having a fresh, slightly citrus taste. Due to variations in the gene OR6A2, some people perceive its flavor to be more soaplike or rotten."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-coriander-leaf"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coriander_leaf"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine",[["Panipuri","dish/panipuri"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Atta (flour)","ingredient/atta-flour"],["Chaat masala","ingredient/chaat-masala"],["Chickpea","ingredient/chickpea"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Semolina","ingredient/semolina"],["Tamarind","ingredient/tamarind"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/1/13/Coriandrum_sativum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-193.jpg","w":500,"h":630,"by":"Franz Eugen Köhler, Köhler's Medizinal-Pflanzen","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coriandrum_sativum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-193.jpg"}},"ingredient/curing-food-preservation":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/curing-food-preservation","n":"Curing (food preservation)","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Curing is any of various food preservation and flavoring processes of foods such as meat, fish and vegetables, by the addition of salt, with the aim of drawing moisture out of the food by the process of osmosis. Because curing increases the solute concentration in the food and hence decreases its water potential, the food becomes inhospitable for the microbe growth that causes food spoilage. Curing can be traced back to antiquity, and was the primary method of preserving meat and fish until the late 19th century. Dehydration was the earliest form of food curing.  Many curing processes also involve smoking, spicing, cooking, or the addition of combinations of sugar, nitrate, and nitrite."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-curing-food-preservation"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Curing_(food_preservation)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine",[["Pickled egg","dish/pickled-egg"]]],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine",[["Pickled egg","dish/pickled-egg"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Brine","ingredient/brine"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Hard-boiled egg","ingredient/hard-boiled-egg"],["Vinegar","ingredient/vinegar"]]}]},"dish/tutto-italia-ristorante":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/tutto-italia-ristorante","n":"Tutto Italia Ristorante","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Italian cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["The Italy Pavilion is an Italian-themed pavilion that is part of the World Showcase, within Epcot at Walt Disney World in Bay Lake, Florida, United States. Its location is between the Germany pavilion and The American Adventure."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-tutto-italia-ristorante"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tutto_Italia_Ristorante"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Pasta Grannies","ref":"site/pastagrannies-substack-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"pastagrannies.substack.com","note":"Vicky Bennison's long-running video series filming Italian grandmothers making regional pasta by hand.","url":"https://pastagrannies.substack.com/p/pasta-grannies-is-now-on-substack","suggested":true},{"name":"Recipes from Italy","ref":"site/recipesfromitaly-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"recipesfromitaly.com","note":"Traditional Italian recipes written by Italian home cooks, organized by region and course.","url":"https://recipesfromitaly.com","suggested":true},{"name":"Manu's Menu","ref":"site/manusmenu-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"manusmenu.com","note":"Italian-born home cook Manuela sharing classic family and regional Italian dishes.","url":"https://www.manusmenu.com/about/about-this-site","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from Italian cuisine","links":[["8½ Otto e Mezzo Bombana","dish/8-otto-e-mezzo-bombana"],["A16 (restaurant)","dish/a16-restaurant"],["Acquacotta","dish/acquacotta"],["Agliata","dish/agliata"],["Agnolotti","dish/agnolotti"],["Alfredo alla Scrofa","dish/alfredo-alla-scrofa"],["Alioto's","dish/alioto-s"],["Amalfi's Italian Restaurant","dish/amalfi-s-italian-restaurant"],["Amato's","dish/amato-s"],["Angelo's Civita Farnese","dish/angelo-s-civita-farnese"],["Antica Pizzeria Port'Alba","dish/antica-pizzeria-port-alba"],["Antico Pizza","dish/antico-pizza"],["Antipasto","dish/antipasto"],["Arancini","dish/arancini"],["ASK Italian","dish/ask-italian"],["Asti (restau